Today — Async JS
This is the last major JS concept before React. And it's critical — every API call, every database query, every real-world operation is async.

Why Async Exists — Under the Hood
JavaScript is single threaded. One thing at a time. If fetching data took 3 seconds and JS just waited — your entire page would freeze for 3 seconds. Nothing could click, scroll, or move.
So JS says: "Start this task, move on, come back when it's done."
That's async. The mechanism that makes this work is called the Event Loop — but you don't need to master that today. Just understand the concept.

Promises — what they are
A Promise is JS saying: "I don't have this value yet, but I promise I'll give it to you when it's ready."
A Promise has 3 states:

Pending — still waiting
Resolved — got the value
Rejected — something went wrong

javascript

const promise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  const success = true

  if (success) {
    resolve("Here is your data")
  } else {
    reject("Something went wrong")
  }
})

promise
  .then(data => console.log(data))    // runs if resolved
  .catch(error => console.log(error)) // runs if rejected

async/await — cleaner syntax for Promises
async/await doesn't replace Promises. It's just cleaner syntax on top of them. Under the hood it's still Promises.
javascript// 
This:
fetch(url)
  .then(res => res.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data))
  .catch(err => console.log(err))

// Is the same as this:
async function getData() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(url)
    const data = await res.json()
    console.log(data)
  } catch (err) {
    console.log(err)
  }
}
await pauses only that function, not the whole program. Everything else keeps running.
try/catch is not optional. If you don't handle errors, they fail silently and you'll spend hours debugging.